    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and the full build passes. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nAlongside the existing human-readable audit line (`AuditOwnerCreated`), `POST /api/owners` now emits an immutable structured event to the `AUDIT` logger:\n\n```\n{\"seq\":1,\"ownerId\":11,\"customerCode\":\"NSW-16E6609E\",\"membershipLevel\":1,\"event\":\"OWNER_CREATED\"}\n```\n\nI kept each responsibility in its own small unit rather than growing `AuditOwnerCreated` into a catch-all:\n\n- **`OwnerCreatedEvent`** (record) \u2014 the immutable event `{seq, ownerId, customerCode, membershipLevel, event}`, with an `of(seq, owner)` factory that stamps `event = \"OWNER_CREATED\"`.\n- **`OwnerIdentity.primary(owner)`** \u2014 the single source of truth for the owner's *primary identifier*. It returns `customerCode` today; when that identifier is unified into the memberId, this one method changes and the event carries the memberId automatically. This is exactly the future-proofing the spec calls for.\n- **`OwnerCreatedEventPublisher`** (`@Component`) \u2014 a Spring singleton owning the shared monotonic `AtomicLong` sequence (so `seq` increases across creates for the app lifetime) plus JSON serialization (via the injected Jackson 3 `ObjectMapper`) and the AUDIT emission.\n- **`AuditOwnerCreatedEvent`** \u2014 a one-method pipeline step (`@Val Owner`, injected publisher) that simply delegates to the publisher, keeping the structured event separate from the human-readable line.\n\nWiring: the `owners.POST.yml` pipeline now runs `audit \u2192 auditEvent \u2192 respond`, with the new step under the same `govern: [ transaction ]` as its neighbors.\n\nThe event carries the owner's persisted id and its current primary identifier (customerCode), satisfying `coreEventCarriesIdAndIdentifier` and the full suite (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`, `BUILD SUCCESS`).",
